{{$NEXT}}

  - Requires Alien::Tree::Sitter 0.001: the tree-sitter headers for the
    grammar build come from there, and the grammar is built once per
    process and shared by every instance
  - parse() croaks with a clear message on invalid UTF-8 and on NUL
    bytes, and reports the compiler output when the grammar build fails
  - commands() returns a command wherever Bash executes one: inside
    $(...), backticks and <(...) / >(...) in assignments (x=$(cmd),
    X=$(cmd) command, arrays, subscripts), export / declare / local /
    readonly / typeset / unset, [ ], [[ ]] and (( )), the command word,
    redirect targets, herestrings and heredoc bodies with an unquoted
    delimiter. The pipeline or list behind a heredoc operator
    (cat <<EOF | sh) is extracted with before_op / after_op set
  - commands() keeps every argument on tree-sitter 0.20.x libraries,
    Debian's libtree-sitter included
  - commands() takes the argv of export, declare, unset and test from
    the syntax tree, so export FOO="hello world" stays one argument
    and declare -i x=1 keeps -i
  - New command fields: negated => 1 for commands under !, test => 1
    for [ ] and [[ ]], and function => 1 for function definitions,
    which appear as entries of their own. The enclosing if, for, while
    and case appear in context. A redirect is recorded on the command
    it belongs to, and before_op / after_op connect commands of the
    same level
  - New parse_errors($source) returns the regions tree-sitter could not
    parse. findings() reports each as a parse_error finding, and
    Security::Checker->check_source as an issue of rule ParseError with
    severity high, so "no findings" means the source parsed completely.
    Security::Checker->new( parse_errors => 0 ) switches this off
  - New finding type unresolved_command for a command word that is only
    an expansion ($SHELL -c id, ${cmd}, $(which sh))
  - findings() classifies by the command that really runs: sudo, doas,
    env, exec, nohup, command, time, nice and xargs are looked through,
    and quotes and escapes in the command word are removed
    (\bash, b"a"sh), so curl x | sudo bash and xargs sh -c id are
    reported like the bare shell
  - findings() reads short-option clusters for dynamic_shell
    (bash -lc, sh -ec, perl -le, python3 -Bc, node -pe) and recognises
    csh, tcsh, ash, busybox, python variants such as pypy, jython and
    micropython, and nushell
  - network_to_shell fires when a substitution sits between the fetcher
    and the pipe (curl "http://x/$(hostname)" | sh) and for |&; a ||
    between fetcher and shell does not fire it
  - Security::Checker croaks when a rule class or object has no check
    method, and ignores rule results that are not hashes
  - New rules: SensitiveRedirect, InsecureDownload, NetworkListener,
    ForkBomb, PrivilegeEscalation, IFSManipulation, DangerousExpansion,
    ReverseShellSink and DangerousFilesystem
  - DangerousFlags is limited to the commands where the flags matter
    (rm, mv, cp, find, chmod, chown, chgrp, rsync, tar, zip, unzip,
    git), needs both force and recursive, and matches short flags
    case-insensitively and inside clusters (-rf, -fR, -RF)
  - SensitiveAccess covers more credential stores (.docker/config.json,
    .gnupg/private-keys, .git-credentials, .netrc, .pypirc, .npmrc,
    .cargo/credentials, .pgpass, gcloud, azure and gh configs,
    browser profiles, macOS Keychain, the Docker socket, kernel module
    paths, /proc/*/environ, /etc/gshadow, /etc/sudoers.d/) and lets
    harmless /dev/null, /dev/zero, /dev/std*, /dev/*random and
    /dev/fd* through
  - EnvDangerousVars covers IFS, PROMPT_COMMAND, PS4, SHELLOPTS,
    BASH_FUNC_*, the interpreter library and preload variables
    (PYTHONPATH, PYTHONSTARTUP, NODE_PATH, NODE_OPTIONS, PERL5LIB,
    PERL5OPT, RUBYLIB, RUBYOPT, CLASSPATH, LD_LIBRARY_PATH) and
    GIT_WORK_TREE / GIT_INDEX_FILE. It and UnquotedExpansion look at
    the text of their own command only, so a variable name that is
    merely mentioned (echo ENV, grep IFS file) does not fire
  - UnquotedExpansion recognises ${...} and $((...)), reports the
    variable with its leading $, ignores quotes, comments, heredoc
    bodies, [[ ]] and assignment values (DIR=$HOME/bin), and reports a
    command nested in $(...) once, for itself
  - MissingAbsolutePath reports relative paths such as foo/bar, allows
    shell builtins and common package managers and build tools, and
    skips words that are no command names
  - PathTraversal matches any .. path segment
  - Security::Rule->mask_source($source) is a new helper for rules that
    match on the text of one command
  - POD for every module, a LIMITATIONS section for the substitutions
    the 0.20.5 grammar does not parse, and docs/SECURITY.md and
    docs/RULES.md with the rule catalogue

0.001     2026-04-24 02:54:50Z

  - Initial release
  - Vendor tree-sitter-bash 0.20.5 grammar sources
  - Add Bash command extraction and security findings
  - Add Text::Treesitter::Bash::Security::Checker with rules:
    PathTraversal, DangerousFlags, SensitiveAccess, EnvDangerousVars,
    UnquotedExpansion, MissingAbsolutePath
